    <h2>Reflection is the engine</h2>
    <p>
        Across the literature, <strong>reflection sits at the core of portfolio learning</strong>
        (Yancey, 2009). Reflection is what drives <strong>metacognition</strong>, i.e. "thinking about your
        own thinking", so that students become aware of <em>how</em> they learn and how their
        understanding has changed over time. That awareness is associated with higher-order thinking and
        deeper learning, not just the recall of content (Wolf et al., 2022). The artefact
        shows <em>what</em> you did; the reflection beside it shows what it <em>means</em>, and that is
        where the learning happens.
    </p>

    <h2>Integration: making meaning across courses</h2>
    <p>
        Because artefacts in a portfolio are not locked to a single course, students can
        <strong>synthesise and integrate learning across courses and experiences to find meaning</strong>
        in the whole, not just the parts. This integrative move is one of the clearest benefits of the
        ePortfolio: it asks the learner to stand back and ask "what does all of this add up to?", which is
        exactly the question a capstone or exit portfolio is built around (see the
        <a href="{{{links.usecase_programme}}}">programme exit portfolio</a> walkthrough).
    </p>

    <h2>Two faces: portfolio <em>for</em> learning and portfolio <em>of</em> learning</h2>
    <p>
        Portfolios serve more than one purpose, and good practice begins by being clear about which one
        you mean. Researchers distinguish portfolios used for <strong>learning</strong>, for
        <strong>assessment</strong>, and for <strong>professional development</strong>, and warn that
        conflating these purposes weakens all of them (Smith and Tillema, 2006). Barrett (2010) frames
        the same tension as the <strong>"two faces" of the ePortfolio</strong>: the
        <em>workspace</em> (a reflective process - the portfolio <em>for</em> learning) and the
        <em>showcase</em> (a polished, public product - the portfolio <em>of</em> learning). Byblos
        supports both: keep a page in <a href="{{{links.publishing}}}">draft</a> while it is a workspace,
        then publish and <a href="{{{links.sharing}}}">share</a> it when it becomes a showcase.
    </p>

    <h2>Ownership, agency and identity</h2>
    <p>
        When students choose what to include and how to present it, the portfolio becomes
        <em>theirs</em>., increasing their sense of <strong>ownership, agency and relevance</strong>. The
        act of curating and narrating one's own learning is also an act of building a
        scholarly and professional <strong>identity</strong>: a learner deciding "this is who I am
        becoming, and here is the evidence." The <a href="{{{links.usecase_professional}}}">professional
        showcase</a> leans hardest on this idea.
    </p>

    <h2>Why it counts as a high-impact practice</h2>
    <p>
        The AAC&amp;U introduced the idea of <strong>High-Impact Practices (HIPs)</strong> in 2007, teaching and learning approaches that reliably deepen learning and improve outcomes such as
        retention and achievement, with especially strong benefits for students from underserved
        backgrounds (Kuh, 2008). In 2016 the ePortfolio was added as the <strong>eleventh HIP</strong>
        (Watson et al., 2016). Kuh and O'Donnell (2013) identify
        <strong>eight elements</strong> that make any practice high-impact:
    </p>
    <ul class="byblos-docs-deflist">
        <li>high performance expectations;</li>
        <li>significant time and effort over an extended period;</li>
        <li>substantive interaction with staff and peers;</li>
        <li>experience of diversity and difference;</li>
        <li>frequent, timely, constructive <strong>feedback</strong>;</li>
        <li>periodic, structured <strong>reflection</strong> and integration of learning;</li>
        <li>real-world application; and</li>
        <li><strong>public demonstration</strong> of competence.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        A well-run ePortfolio can embody almost all eight at once, which is why it is sometimes called a
        "meta-HIP" that amplifies the others. Eynon and Gambino's (2017) study of effective practice
        distils the design into three moves that recur throughout this documentation:
        <strong>Inquiry, Reflection and Integration</strong>.
    </p>

    <h2>The theory underneath</h2>
    <p>
        These ideas rest on a long tradition. Experiential learning theory holds that we learn by acting
        and then reflecting on the result (Kolb, 1984), building on Dewey's (1933) account of reflective
        thought and Schön's (1983) "reflective practitioner" who reflects both in and on their action. A
        portfolio operationalises that cycle: do the work, gather the evidence, reflect, and feed what you
        notice back into what you do next. This is supported, in competency-based settings, by feedback at
        milestones that turns reflection into formative assessment (Wolf et al., 2022).
    </p>

    <h2>How Byblos puts this into practice</h2>
    <ul class="byblos-docs-deflist">
        <li><strong>Collect &amp; curate</strong> - artefacts, pages and sections let students gather evidence from across courses and choose what best represents them.</li>
        <li><strong>Reflect</strong> - text sections sit alongside evidence so every artefact can carry its "so what".</li>
        <li><strong>Integrate</strong> - <a href="{{{links.collections}}}">collections</a> and a home page let a learner tell one coherent story across many pages.</li>
        <li><strong>Feedback &amp; interaction</strong> - <a href="{{{links.sharing}}}">sharing</a> and peer review build in the substantive feedback that HIPs depend on.</li>
        <li><strong>Public demonstration</strong> - publishing, course/cohort sharing and public links let students demonstrate competence to a real audience, from a teacher to an employer.</li>
    </ul>

